@@ -27,6 +27,33 @@ parsePointer("/a~1b/0");                           // ["a/b", "0"]
 
 Only the JSON string representation is accepted; a URI fragment (`#/foo`) must be decoded first.
 
+### JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch, JsonPatchError } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: ["bar"] }, [
+  { op: "add", path: "/foo/-", value: "baz" },          // append to an array
+  { op: "move", from: "/foo/0", path: "/first" },
+  { op: "test", path: "/foo", value: ["baz"] },
+]); // { foo: ["baz"], first: "bar" }
+```
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` supports the six operations `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and
+`test`, and returns a new document: neither `document` nor `patch` is modified, and the result shares no
+object or array with them. A patch applies atomically: if any operation fails, it throws and no partial
+result exists.
+
+It throws a `JsonPatchError` (whose `index` property is the position of the operation at fault) when:
+
+- the patch is invalid: not an array, or an operation that is not an object, has an unknown `op`, or
+  lacks a valid `path`, `from` (for `move` and `copy`) or `value` (for `add`, `replace` and `test`).
+  The whole patch is checked before any operation is applied; unknown members are ignored;
+- an operation fails: a target or `from` location that does not exist (pointers resolve as in
+  `resolvePointer`; `add` also accepts `-` and an index equal to the array length, to append), a `move`
+  into one of the value's own children, a `test` whose value differs (objects compare regardless of
+  member order), or a `remove` of the whole document. A pointer error is kept as the error's `cause`.
+
 ## Development
 
 `npm test` runs the tests with Node's test runner (Node 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
