@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-api-atomic-copy-on-apply-upfront-validation-jsonpatcherror-with-index
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch API: atomic copy-on-apply, upfront validation, JsonPatchError with index"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Services need RFC 6902 JSON Patch (`applyPatch(document, patch)` in `src/index.ts`), returning a new
+document without mutating its input and throwing on an invalid patch or a failing operation. RFC 6902
+leaves open how errors are reported, when validation happens, whether a failed patch leaves partial
+changes, and what removing the whole document means. It builds on the JSON Pointer code of
+dl-001-json-pointer-api-string-form-only-jsonpointererror-strict-array-indexes (still pending).
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **Copy, then apply in place.** The document is deep-copied once; operations change that copy, and
+   each inserted `value` (and each `copy` source) is deep-copied too. The result shares nothing with
+   the input or the patch, and a failing patch leaves no partial result (atomic, RFC 6902 section 5).
+2. **Validate the whole patch first.** Shape errors (not an array, non-object operation, unknown `op`,
+   missing/invalid `path`/`from`, missing `value`) are reported before any operation runs. A `value`
+   member holding `undefined` counts as missing. Unknown members are ignored (section 4).
+3. **One error class, `JsonPatchError`,** with `index` (position of the operation at fault,
+   `undefined` when the patch is not an array) and the `JsonPointerError` as `cause` when a pointer does
+   not resolve. Pointer resolution reuses `resolvePointer`/`parsePointer` strictly (own members only,
+   no `-` except as an `add` target).
+4. **`remove` of the root (`""`) is an error**: there is no "no document" value to return. `add` and
+   `replace` at `""` replace the whole document; `move` with `from === path` is a no-op.
+5. **Object members are set with `Object.defineProperty`** and cloned with `Object.fromEntries`, so a
+   `__proto__` key is an ordinary own member and can never pollute a prototype.
+6. `patch` is typed `unknown`: patches typically arrive as parsed JSON and validation is the function's
+   job. A `JsonPatchOperation` type is exported for callers who build patches.
+7. Code stays in `src/index.ts`, for the same reason as dl-001 point 6.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Cost is one full copy of the document per call, acceptable for configuration files and payloads.
+- Callers can tell which operation failed (`error.index`) and distinguish patch errors by class.
+- A patch with a malformed later operation fails even if earlier ones would have failed differently.
